  "question": {
    "text": "The line passing through the extremity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> of the major axis and extremity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> of the minor axis of the ellipse <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></math> meets its auxiliary circle at the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math>. Then, the area of the triangle with vertices at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>M</mi></math> and the origin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi></math> is",
    "image": null
  },
